[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: A plan's backlog is a register node, and items point at it

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[DECISION]
UID: DEC-BACKLOG-IS-A-REGISTER-NODE
TITLE: A plan's backlog is a register node, and items point at it
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATUS: accepted
RETIRES_ON: >>>
Milestone five gives items real semantics and the register turns out to be
carrying something the item types should carry themselves, or a second axis of
containment appears that one Backlogged_In edge per item cannot express.
<<<
STATEMENT: >>>
Every plan directory holds a backlog REGISTER: one node, in its own file, that
the plan's ungroomed items point at with Backlogged_In. The register holds no
list; the edges are the list, and the backlog is the register's inbound Backlogs
set.

An item may carry other edges at the same time -- Governed_By, Crosses, Assumes,
whatever it actually relates to. Containment by edge does not consume the item's
only parent, so an item can be in the backlog and in the graph at once.

This supersedes containment by DOCUMENT. It does not change what an item IS: an
ungroomed item is still an ordinary node at DEPTH sketch, there is still no
BACKLOG node type, and grooming still raises DEPTH.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
DEC-PLAN-SCOPED-BACKLOG named this successor in its own retirement condition --
an item needing to be tracked while living outside its plan's backlog document
is the point at which an explicit register node and a Backlogged_In role become
the cheaper answer. The one-node-per-file carve put every item outside that
document, so the condition fired rather than being reinterpreted.

The intermediate state the carve left behind was a directory query, DEPTH ==
sketch scoped to a plan's directory. It answers correctly and it is not in the
graph: it is a fact about where bytes sit, invisible to anything traversing
edges, and unavailable to a reader who has a node and follows its relations. The
whole point of the carve was to make the fetchable unit the addressable unit;
leaving containment outside the edges would have moved the same problem up a
level.

An edge also composes where both a file and a directory query did not. A file
forces exactly one containment, since a node sits in one file; the query forces
one more, since a node sits in one directory. An edge is additive.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Ruled by the operator 2026-08-27, immediately after the carve, verbatim: "having
the 99 is fine, but its the lynchpin node. every issue under it parents that.
issues may parent other nodes that are related, but thats the idea, a web" and
"i mean 99 is still a file, the node has to exist some place."

Scope, stated because the operator stated it: "we havent fully designed
semantics yet, thats milestone 5 in the strictdoc plan, this is jsut an
intermediate carbe out." So this decision is deliberately narrow. What kinds of
item there are, what grooming promotes an item INTO, and what closes one are
milestone five's questions and are not answered here. What is settled is only
where an item is filed and how.

Applied the same day: MECH-BACKLOG-STRICTDOC-TOOLING and
MECH-BACKLOG-BEADS-MIGRATION exist, and all 81 nodes at DEPTH sketch carry
Backlogged_In to their own plan's register. Verified as a set equality in both
directions -- no sketch node unfiled, no filed node above sketch -- and nothing
enforces it yet; MECH-SDOC-LAYOUT-CHECK is where that guard belongs.

The Backlogged_In / Backlogs role pair was added to all five node types in
packages/strictdoc-grammar/values.nix and the .sgra regenerated. Every type
carries it because anything can be noticed before it is groomed, including a
decision nobody has made yet.
<<<
